  "id": "COMPOUND:14444 - PROTEIN:6340",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that lidocaine is associated with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through a series of target relationships. Specifically, lidocaine is linked to the CD70 antigen, which is then targeted by a compound with the structure (2S)-1-O-3,6,9,12,15-Octadecapentaenoyl-2-O-6,9,12,15-Octadecatetraenoyl-3-O-Beta-D-Galactopyranosylsn-Glycerol, and this compound further targets the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. Another pathway connects lidocaine to CD70 antigen via a different compound, Gibberellin A19, which also targets the same complex. These findings indicate that lidocaine may influence the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through the modulation of CD70 antigen activity, potentially via a signaling pathway involving lipid-derived molecules. The evidence suggests a possible role for lidocaine in modulating the activity of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The strength of the evidence is moderate, as multiple pathways are involved, and the relationships are indirect, highlighting the need for further experimental validation.",
